From 3cfd2153c058dfaa83e761e92b1957569c52b126 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: amd-weisun <Wei.Sun@amd.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 15:11:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Serve the FP8 paged-attention decode from aiter on gfx950
 where it is faster

paged_attention_decode_fp8 now picks per shape between MSLK's FlyDSL kernel
and aiter's FlyDSL pa_decode, which takes the op's cache layout, scales and
block tables as they are. aiter's ASM pa_fwd_asm is not a candidate: its
softmax scale is fixed to 1/sqrt(128), it takes only block size 16, and it was
slower than both.

- mslk/attention/aiter/paged_attention_decode_fp8.py: builds aiter's work plan
  on the GPU and calls pa_decode; aiter uses its own scratch.
- mslk/attention/dispatch/paged_attention_decode_fp8.py: the backends, which
  calls aiter takes, and the table key: heads, head size, block size and the
  context the block table holds (rounded up to a power of two), with the batch
  size as the bucket. All come from shapes, so a CUDA graph replays the backend
  it was captured with.
- bench/aiter/tune_paged_attention_decode_fp8.py and its shape list; the table
  in mslk/attention/dispatch/tables/. aiter serves 323 of 1008 rows.
- Tests: the op's tests run through the routing and with each backend forced,
  plus shapes the table sends to each backend and a call aiter cannot take;
  test/attention/dispatch_test.py and the lazy-import test gain the op.
  package_data, the ROCm CI path filter and the CI test list gain the files.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
